            <ArticleTitle>Monitoring sedentary patterns in office employees: validity of an m-health tool (Walk@Work-App) for occupational health.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0213-9111(17)30140-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.gaceta.2017.05.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study validated the Walk@Work-Application (W@W-App) for measuring occupational sitting and stepping.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The W@W-App was installed on the smartphones of office-based employees (n=17; 10 women; 26±3 years). A prescribed 1-hour laboratory protocol plus two continuous hours of occupational free-living activities were performed. Intra-class correlation coefficients (ICC) compared mean differences of sitting time and step count measurements between the W@W-App and criterion measures (ActivPAL3TM and SW200Yamax Digi-Walker).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During the protocol, agreement between self-paced walking (ICC=0.85) and active working tasks step counts (ICC=0.80) was good. The smallest median difference was for sitting time (1.5seconds). During free-living conditions, sitting time (ICC=0.99) and stepping (ICC=0.92) showed excellent agreement, with a difference of 0.5minutes and 18 steps respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The W@W-App provided valid measures for monitoring occupational sedentary patterns in real life conditions; a key issue for increasing awareness and changing occupational sedentariness.</AbstractText>
